# Bounded original Half contraction/projection probe

This draft isolates two missing native FP16 contracts: original
`cc_vit_1d_ffn_contract` and `cc_vit_1d_projection` from the unchanged
module-5 SM120 cubin. It is a correctness experiment using Half records derived
from the recovered checkpoint. It does not execute the DLL host, recover its
weight-preparation code, time either implementation or validate the full graph.
No GPU execution has occurred for this draft.

The permitted shape is **batch 1, 128 tokens, 1,024 output channels**, with
block `(32,4,1)` and grid `(8,1,4)`. Each output tile has four K partitions.
Contraction uses K4096/partition1024; projection uses K1024/partition256. Other
shapes, tails and batch counts are rejected. The first worker also requires
at least 32 SMs and capacity for all 32 CTAs according to the actual original
function's occupancy query. The caller must provide exclusive GPU work; this
eligibility check is not a general scheduling guarantee.

| Parameter byte offset | Contract |
|---|---|
| 0 / 8 / 16 | Disjoint input / skip / output Half buffers |
| 24 | Derived K16/N16 Half matrix, followed by 1,024 Half scales |
| 32 | Eight signed int32 completion counters |
| 40 / 48 / 56 | Unused and set to zero; **no external scratch** |
| 64 / 68 | int32 batch=1 / tokens=128 |

Input allocations are 1,048,576 bytes for contraction and 262,144 for projection.
Skip/output each use 262,144 bytes; weight-plus-scale records use 8,390,656 /
2,099,200 bytes; counters use 32 bytes. All have external guard regions. The
CPU address proof interprets the exact original PTX and checks actual global
accesses, logical Half-fragment maps, per-partition K bounds and output
coverage across 4,096 thread instances per entry. It models successful async
copy completion and predecessor-counter readiness; it does not emulate MMA
arithmetic or prove device-wide synchronization.

Every counter starts at **âˆ’1 before every launch**, including every captured
graph replay. Partition 0 seeds its accumulator with Half(skipÃ—scale), computes
its K slice and stores. Partitions 1/2 perform Half reductions into output;
partition 3 loads output, adds its partial in Half and stores. Each partition
then release-stores its number. A later partition polls for its predecessor.
Resetting to zero or retaining a previous final value is invalid.

The original consumer uses a relaxed counter load and a CTA barrier, followed
by weak cached output loads. Producer fences are present in SASS. This is not
the explicit acquire sequence described by the
[PTX memory model](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/parallel-thread-execution/index.html#release-and-acquire-patterns).
The draft preserves the executable unchanged and tests its behavior empirically;
it neither inserts stronger synchronization nor claims a general memory-model
proof.

The candidate is `ordered_linear(..., fp8=False, partition=K/4)` with baseline
variant 1 explicitly selected and restored afterward. Both sides consume the
same decoded block-31/38 checkpoint values. A separate checkpoint decoder
cross-checks all matrix/scales before launching. Fixtures exercise residual
seeding, nonzero contributions from every partition, and real-weight random
inputs. Each has three distinct operand versions.

The probe retains independent eager results, repeats the original and candidate
with zero/poison-filled outputs, then captures the counter reset plus both
operations. The captured output and original output are filled before replay.
Mutation order `1,2,0` makes three real operand changes from initial version 0;
each must match its preserved eager result byte-for-byte. All supplied
inputs/weights/scales and guards remain immutable, and every counter must end
at 3. Candidate outputs are extension allocations, retained and byte-checked
rather than externally guarded. Graphs are destroyed before their raw native
buffers or module can be released.

`--execute` runs a separate worker with a bounded timeout and no retry. A
timeout report remains failed even if earlier cases passed; it distinguishes
confirmed process exit from unsuccessful termination. Killing the process is
not proof that GPU/context recovery has completed. Stop and inspect after any
timeout, mismatch, guard failure, sanitizer error or incomplete counter.

Static checks (no CUDA):

```powershell
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe tests\test_vendor_fp16_linear_probe_cpu.py.draft
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe tools\vendor_fp16_linear_probe.py.draft --blocks 31 --output outputs\fp16-linear-cpu.json.draft
```

The bounded address proof and independent protocol/runtime review are complete
and pinned in the draft. CPU checks pass; **no native launch has run**. The first
**root-scheduled future smoke** is only block 31 projection/residual seeding:

```powershell
& "C:\Program Files\NVIDIA GPU Computing Toolkit\CUDA\v13.4\bin\compute-sanitizer.bat" --tool memcheck --launch-timeout 60 --error-exitcode 99 --target-processes all --log-file outputs\fp16-linear-first-memcheck.log .\.venv\Scripts\python.exe tools\vendor_fp16_linear_probe.py.draft --execute --blocks 31 --kinds projection --fixtures residual_only --timeout 120 --output outputs\fp16-linear-first.json
```

Only after that result passes should the same guarded worker cover both kinds,
both blocks and all three fixture families, followed by bounded racecheck.
Neither a static `completed` report nor a successful address proof is a native
numerical pass: require `executed_gpu=true`, all expected exact cases/replays,
matching proof/source/binary identities and clean sanitizer logs.


## Exceptional cleanup and isolated execution

The earlier frozen draft only dropped graph references before closing native
resources. That was unsafe when a `capture_end` traceback retained the graph.
The original files and manifests remain byte-for-byte in
`outputs/native-probe-cleanup-review/pre-fix/`.

The revised draft registers every graph before capture and retains all raw
allocations, captured outputs, images, textures, and module owners together.
Successful teardown synchronizes, explicitly calls `reset()` on every graph,
and only then closes native resources in dependency order. A capture body or
capture-end failure leaves completion uncertain. Synchronization, graph-reset,
or resource-close failure also makes teardown uncertain. In those cases the
whole owner is retained; no dependent module unload is attempted. The original
failure stays primary, with cleanup failures attached as notes.

The CLI runs native execution in a fresh worker. On quarantine, the worker
writes its failed report, prints the original traceback, and exits without
Python CUDA-object teardown. The parent enforces a finite timeout and does not
retry. Confirmed worker exit does not establish GPU/context recovery; stop and
inspect after any timeout or cleanup failure. CPU failure-injection tests
exercise retained capture tracebacks, double failures, synchronization/reset/
close failures, and stale or incomplete worker reports. No CUDA execution was
used to validate this cleanup revision.

Stream-context restoration also preserves a pending body error and is skipped after uncertain capture. Diagnostic printing or stream flushing cannot bypass the worker hard-exit. The cleanup regression suite has17 tests; together with15 FP16 and17 full-chain tests, all49 pass normally and under `-O`. Both CPU preflights passed without native CUDA execution.
